            On September 19, 1981, legendary folk-rock duo Paul Simon and Art Garfunkel reunited for a massive free benefit concert on the Great Lawn of New York City's Central Park. Organized to raise awareness and vital funds for the maintenance and restoration of the deteriorating park, the historic event drew an estimated 500,000 attendees.
        </p>

        <p>
            Backed by an exceptional eleven-piece ensemble of studio musicians, the duo delivered transcendent live renditions of their timeless catalog alongside select solo works. The concert captured the peak of their vocal harmony and cemented its place as one of the largest and most culturally significant live music events in American history.
        </p>

        <h3 style="color:#66ccff;margin-top:25px;margin-bottom:10px;">
            Setlist Highlights & Performance Style
        </h3>

        <p>
            The unforgettable setlist seamlessly blended acoustic classics like "Mrs. Robinson", "Homeward Bound", "Scarborough Fair", and "The Sound of Silence" with energized full-band arrangements. Solo highlights included Garfunkel's emotive delivery of "A Heart in New York" and Simon's dynamic performances of "Late in the Evening" and "Still Crazy After All These Years".
        </p>

